<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>For each statement, choose true or false.</p><p>(a) Doubling the speed of a current at right angles to the course doubles the drift angle. [ true | false ]  </p><p>(b) When there is no current, the track is the same as the course. [ true | false ]  </p><p>(c) The ground speed is always more than the speed through the water. [ true | false ]  </p><p>(d) A wind blowing from <m>270^\circ</m> pushes an aircraft towards the east. [ true | false ]  </p><p>(e) The ground speed is found by adding the boat's speed and the current's speed. [ true | false ]  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>The course is where the boat points, the track is where it goes, and the drift angle is the angle between them. Velocities add as arrows: course velocity <m>+</m> current velocity <m>=</m> track velocity.</p><ul><li>(a) False. <m>\tan d</m> doubles, not <m>d</m> itself: for example <m>\tan^{-1}(0.2) = 11.3^\circ</m> but <m>\tan^{-1}(0.4) = 21.8^\circ</m>.</li><li>(b) True. With no current the velocity triangle collapses: the boat goes where it points.</li><li>(c) False. A current against the boat, or across it at a wide angle, can make the ground speed smaller.</li><li>(d) True. Winds are named by where they come from: from <m>270^\circ</m> (west) means towards <m>090^\circ</m> (east).</li><li>(e) False. Velocities add as arrows, by the triangle; speeds add only when the arrows point the same way.</li></ul></p></solution>
    <answer><p>false</p></answer>
    <answer><p>true</p></answer>
    <answer><p>false</p></answer>
    <answer><p>true</p></answer>
    <answer><p>false</p></answer>
</stack-static>